# 10. Interview Questions and Model Answers

## Q1. What is ITSM?

IT Service Management is an approach to delivering, managing, supporting, and improving IT services to meet business and user needs.

## Q2. What is the difference between an incident and a service request?

An incident is an unplanned interruption or degradation of an IT service. A service request is a request for a standard service, information, access, or an approved item.

## Q3. What is the difference between an incident and a problem?

An incident is the service interruption, while a problem is the underlying cause or potential cause of one or more incidents.

## Q4. What is a change in ITSM?

A change is an addition, modification, or removal that could affect an IT service. Changes are managed through an appropriate process to assess risk, obtain authorization, implement safely, and review the result.

## Q5. What is the difference between impact and urgency?

Impact measures how seriously an issue affects users or business operations. Urgency describes how quickly the issue needs attention. Together with organizational criteria, they help determine priority.

## Q6. What is an SLA?

A Service Level Agreement defines agreed service targets, which may include response time, resolution targets, availability, and service-quality expectations.

## Q7. What would you do if an SLA were at risk?

I would review the priority and deadline, examine the investigation findings, continue authorized troubleshooting, update the user, and escalate early if additional expertise were required. I would document my actions and findings.

## Q8. When should a Help Desk technician escalate a ticket?

I would escalate when the issue exceeds my authorization or technical expertise, involves significant security or business risk, affects a critical service, or requires additional support to meet the applicable service targets.

## Q9. Why is ticket documentation important?

Ticket documentation provides an accurate history of the issue, troubleshooting performed, actions taken, and outcome. It supports continuity, accountability, efficient escalation, and future troubleshooting.

## Q10. What would you do if multiple users reported the same issue?

I would establish the scope, identify the affected users and services, assess business impact and urgency, check for a common cause, and prioritize according to company policy. If a wider outage were suspected, I would promptly notify or escalate to the appropriate team.

